  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The molar conductivity of a conductivity cell filled with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaCl</mi></math> solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#923;</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn></math> moles another identical cell heaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaCl</mi></math>&nbsp;solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#923;</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math>, The conductivities exhibited by these two cells are same.</p><p>The relationship between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#923;</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#923;</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is</p>",
